{"eligibility":"include","exclusion_criterion":"","side_list":"none","eligibility_reason":"The paper evaluates two prompting interventions, self-refinement and RAG, on open-source LLMs and reports quantitative crate hallucination rate (CHR) in generated Rust code. The paper frames this as a supply-chain security risk. Eligibility is borderline because CHR may instead be read as a correctness/reliability measure.","title":"When LLMs Invent Rust Crates: An Empirical Study of Hallucination Patterns and Mitigation","year":2026,"venue":"preprint","venue_type":"preprint","study_type":"technique-evaluation","techniques":[{"technique_name":"Verification-first self-refinement (self-check of crate names, regenerate)","family_v0":"self-refinement","stage":"post-generation-loop","signal_source":["self"],"tools":"","iterations":"up to 3 regenerations if model judges crate hallucinated","dims_targeted":["security"],"dims_measured":["security"],"correctness_measured":false,"metrics":"security: crate hallucination rate (CHR) via crates.io name-list lookup [tool-static]","benchmarks":"Custom Rust dataset: 2,794 tasks (1,001 Stack Overflow, 793 GitHub RustDoc-derived, 1,000 LLM-generated from crate descriptions)","languages":"Rust","models":"DeepSeek-Coder-V2 236B (open, code); OpenCoder 8B (open, code); Qwen2.5-Coder 32B (open, code)","baselines":"No mitigation; RAG","effect_target":"+","effect_target_headline":"DeepSeek 21.92% -> 18.66% (-3.26%); OpenCoder 19.28% -> 17.06% (-2.22%); Qwen2.5 20.07% -> 17.76% (-2.31%)","effect_correctness":"NR","effect_correctness_headline":"","effect_other_dims":{"readability-maintainability":"NR","efficiency":"NR","security":"targeted"}},{"technique_name":"RAG over top-10,000 crates.io names/descriptions plus STD list","family_v0":"retrieval","stage":"pre-generation","signal_source":["retrieved-knowledge"],"tools":"","iterations":"","dims_targeted":["security"],"dims_measured":["security"],"correctness_measured":false,"metrics":"security: CHR [tool-static]","benchmarks":"Same 2,794-task Rust dataset","languages":"Rust","models":"DeepSeek-Coder-V2 236B; OpenCoder 8B; Qwen2.5-Coder 32B","baselines":"No mitigation","effect_target":"mixed","effect_target_headline":"DeepSeek 20.0% (-1.92%); OpenCoder 18.32% (-0.96%); Qwen2.5 20.41% (+0.34%)","effect_correctness":"NR","effect_correctness_headline":"","effect_other_dims":{"readability-maintainability":"NR","efficiency":"NR","security":"targeted"}}],"compute_reported":["none"],"contamination_handled":"no","human_eval":"none","human_eval_details":"","artifact_url":"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.32233767","reimplementable":"partial","qa":{"QA1":0.5,"QA2":1,"QA3":0.5,"QA4":0,"QA5":1,"QA6":0,"QA7":0,"QA8":1,"QA9":0,"QA10":1},"confidence":"low","notes":"Borderline: package/crate hallucination is treated as a security dimension. The authors note CHR is a source-level indicator, not direct security evidence, so a reviewer could instead classify this as correctness-only. Most of the paper is empirical characterization (RQ1–RQ2); mitigation is only RQ3, and statistical tests are reported only for RQ1, not for the mitigation results. Code quality/correctness was not measured despite the abstract's claim of 'without sacrificing code quality'."}
