{"eligibility":"exclude","exclusion_criterion":"IC3","side_list":"correctness-only","eligibility_reason":"This RL technique for LLM website generation targets functional success, rendering validity and VLM-judged visual aesthetics of the rendered UI; none of these is a code-level NF dimension (readability-maintainability, efficiency, security). The only code-level static metric, LDPR (ESLint plus dependency resolution pass rate), appears only in a group-size ablation figure, with no values in the text and no comparison against the base model or baselines.","title":"WebGen-R1: Incentivizing Large Language Models to Generate Functional and Aesthetic Websites with Reinforcement Learning","year":2026,"venue":"preprint","venue_type":"","study_type":"","techniques":"","compute_reported":"","contamination_handled":"","human_eval":"","human_eval_details":"","artifact_url":"","reimplementable":"","qa":"","confidence":"low","notes":"Borderline exclusion. A reviewer should decide two points: (1) whether the ESLint-based LDPR, shown only in Figure 5 for the GRPO group-size ablation, counts as a readability-maintainability outcome; (2) whether visual aesthetics of the rendered website (AAS, VLM-judged with GPT-4o) is in scope for the review. If either is accepted, the paper would be a training-time technique: SFT warm-up plus GRPO on Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct, evaluated on WebGen-Bench (101 tasks) and a 119-task WebDev Arena subset. Code and data: https://github.com/juyongjiang/WebGen-R1."}
